{
  "summary": "I removed the finding recommending acceptance of the 'state and federal' insertion in clause 5.9 because it conflicts with the rejection of the New York governing law/jurisdiction changes; if the jurisdiction clause is reverted to England and Wales, the 'state and federal' wording is inappropriate. I also revised the suggested wording for clause 2.4 to avoid the undefined term 'Affiliates' and to align with the rest of the contract, and I added a knock-on finding for clause 5.6 to flag the need to remove the UK-specific reference if New York law is adopted.",
  "drop": [
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3c1i47b78aa",
      "why": "This finding recommends accepting the insertion of 'state and federal' in clause 5.9, but the governing law and jurisdiction changes to New York are being rejected. If the jurisdiction clause is reverted to England and Wales, the 'state and federal' wording is inappropriate and should be dropped."
    }
  ],
  "revise": [
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3c1aj57e7cb",
      "wording": "For the avoidance of doubt, neither party shall use any Confidential Information, including information retained in the unaided memory of its personnel, for any purpose other than the Purpose.",
      "why": "The original suggested wording was fine, but I revised it to remove the undefined term 'Affiliates' and to make it clearer that the prohibition applies to both parties, aligning with the mutual nature of the agreement."
    }
  ],
  "add": [
    {
      "paragraph_id": "b23",
      "title": "5.6 Third Party Rights",
      "detail": "If the governing law and jurisdiction clauses are reverted to England and Wales, clause 5.6 referencing the UK Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 is consistent. However, if the New York law changes are accepted, this clause would conflict with the New York governing law. Since the New York changes are being rejected, no change is needed here, but if the New York changes were accepted, this clause would need to be amended or removed.",
      "severity": "warn"

  ,
      "wording": "No change needed if the New York law changes are rejected; if accepted, remove or amend clause 5.6 to avoid conflict with New York law."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "injection_suspected": false,
  "injection_notes": ""
}

{
  "summary": "I resolved the conflict between clauses 5.8 and 5.9 by keeping the English governing law and English exclusive jurisdiction (the approved standard) and dropping the New York governing law and New York jurisdiction. I also revised the non-solicitation clause to be mutual and reduced the survival period from 5 to 3 years to match the approved standard. No other contradictions or knock-on effects were found.",
  "drop": [
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3hpyl9b8fa0",
      "why": "It recommends keeping the New York governing law, which conflicts with the approved English jurisdiction in clause 5.9."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3hpylc258cd",
      "why": "It recommends keeping the New York governing law, which conflicts with the approved English jurisdiction in clause 5.9."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3i0b16e321d",
      "why": "It flags a conflict but does not resolve it; the better resolution is to adopt English law and English courts, so this finding is superseded."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3i0b187d202",
      "why": "It suggests amending clause 5.9 to English courts, which is already done; the finding is redundant after the conflict is resolved."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3i3js6f6a7c",
      "why": "It flags a conflict but does not resolve it; the better resolution is to adopt English law and English courts, so this finding is superseded."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3i3js66fbf7",
      "why": "It suggests amending clause 5.8 to English law, which is already done; the finding is redundant after the conflict is resolved."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3i0xa886fdd",
      "why": "It compares the non-solicitation clause to a confidentiality obligation, which is not a valid comparison; the clause is a separate restriction and should be assessed on its own."
    }
  ],
  "revise": [
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3hk3x04eb4a",
      "wording": "This Agreement starts on the date of last signature and continues for two (2) years. The obligations in this Agreement shall continue for three (3) years from the date of termination or expiry, except in relation to trade secrets, which shall be protected for as long as they remain trade secrets.",
      "why": "Reduces the survival period from 5 to 3 years to match the approved standard, as recommended."
    },
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3i0xa6ce87a",
      "wording": "Non-solicitation. During the term of this Agreement and for twelve (12) months after it ends, neither party shall directly or indirectly solicit, employ or engage any employee of the other party with whom it has had contact in connection with the Purpose, provided that this clause shall not prevent either party from advertising generally for staff or from employing any person who responds to such an advertisement.",
      "why": "Makes the non-solicitation mutual and reduces the period from 24 to 12 months, aligning with the approved standard."
    }
  ],
  "add": []
,
  "injection_suspected": false,
  "injection_notes": ""
}

{
  "summary": "The suggested wording for b8 (raising availability to 99.9%) conflicts with the Service Credit table in b16-b17, which has no credit tier for 99.5%-99.9%; I dropped the 99.9% change and kept the original 99.5% target, while retaining the other b8 findings (undefined 'Scheduled Maintenance'). The suggested wording for b9 (30-day cure, no once-per-12-month limit) is kept and does not conflict with other paragraphs. The suggested wording for b13 (30-day payment, VAT, price change) is kept; it does not break other paragraphs. The suggested wording for b20 (12-month lookback, carve-out, tort coverage) is kept; it does not conflict with other paragraphs. No knock-on effects require new findings beyond the existing ones.",
  "drop": [
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3ngcbcb1d90",
      "why": "This finding flags a conflict between the 99.9% target in b8 and the credit table, but the underlying suggested change to 99.9% is not adopted (I keep the original 99.5% target), so the conflict is resolved by not making the change. The finding itself is not dropped as a finding—it correctly identifies the issue—but the suggested change it supports is not applied. To avoid confusion, I note that the 99.9% change is not made, so the conflict does not arise."
    }
  ],
  "revise": [
    {
      "finding_id": "f-mvg3n4bv8e37d4",
      "wording": "The finding's suggested change to 99.9% is not adopted; the original 99.5% target remains. The other aspects of the finding (termination for service level failure only, 60-day cure, once-per-12-month limit) are addressed by the b9 suggested wording, which is adopted.",
      "why": "The 99.9% change would break the Service Credit table (no tier for 99.5%-99.9%), so I keep the original 99.5% target. The termination-related issues are handled by the b9 revision."
    }
  ],
  "add": []
,
